From: Rob Watson <rob@mediaeden.com>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: qcom: spmi-pmic-div: take output names from the device tree

The driver names its outputs div_clk1, div_clk2 and div_clk3. On msm8974 the
RPM clock controller has already registered clocks under those names by the
time this driver probes, so clk_hw_register() returns -EEXIST and the whole
controller fails.

That makes the driver unusable on every msm8974 board, which matters because
the WCD9320 codec on those boards takes its 9.6 MHz master clock from this
divider: the RPM's div_clk1 is an XO buffer fixed at 19.2 MHz and cannot
provide it.

Read the names from the standard clock-output-names property when the device
tree supplies it, and keep the generated names otherwise, so existing device
trees are unaffected.

Signed-off-by: 
---
 drivers/clk/qcom/clk-spmi-pmic-div.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/clk-spmi-pmic-div.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/clk-spmi-pmic-div.c
@@ -243,13 +243,26 @@
 	cxo_hz = clk_get_rate(cxo);
 	clk_put(cxo);
 
-	init.name = name;
 	init.parent_data = &parent_data;
 	init.num_parents = 1;
 	init.ops = &clk_spmi_pmic_div_ops;
 
 	for (i = 0, clkdiv = cc->clks; i < nclks; i++) {
-		snprintf(name, sizeof(name), "div_clk%d", i + 1);
+		const char *of_name;
+
+		/*
+		 * Let the device tree name the outputs. Without this every
+		 * instance registers "div_clk1".."div_clkN", which on msm8974
+		 * collides with the RPM's clocks of the same name and makes
+		 * the whole controller fail to probe with -EEXIST.
+		 */
+		if (!of_property_read_string_index(of_node, "clock-output-names",
+						   i, &of_name)) {
+			init.name = of_name;
+		} else {
+			snprintf(name, sizeof(name), "div_clk%d", i + 1);
+			init.name = name;
+		}
 
 		spin_lock_init(&clkdiv[i].lock);
 		clkdiv[i].base = start + i * 0x100;
